export const GUIDE_MARKDOWN: Record<string, string> = {
  "concepts": "# Sidekick concepts\n\nSidekick is an AI teammate for consulting teams. It reads your project's documents and meeting transcripts, answers questions with sources, writes meeting notes, and keeps track of what the team knows and doesn't. These are the ideas behind it.\n\n## Organization\nYour company's Sidekick account. Everything in it (people, workspaces, documents, meetings) is completely separate from every other organization. Nothing is shared between organizations, ever.\n\n## Group (workspace)\nOne client team, like \"Payments Platform\" for the client Northwind Bank. The portal calls it a **group**; some screens still say workspace, which means the same thing. Each group has a **Team Lead** who runs it day to day. Each workspace has its own documents, meetings, memory and members. **Sidekick only ever searches the workspace you're in**, so an answer for one client can never use another client's material.\n\n## People and roles\n\nThe full rulebook, with every action and which roles may do it, is [Roles and permissions](roles-and-permissions.md).\n| Role | Can do |\n|---|---|\n| **Org Owner** | Everything, including the organization itself |\n| **Org Admin** | People, workspaces, Teams channels, usage and the audit log, across all workspaces |\n| **Team Lead** | Everything a Member can do, plus the people side of the workspaces they lead (the ones they belong to): add people to them as Member or Viewer, take people off them, and link Teams chats to them. Can't create workspaces or organizations, change anyone's role, remove people from the organization, or see other workspaces |\n| **Billing Admin** | Usage and billing only |\n| **Member** | Work inside the workspaces they belong to: upload, import meetings, ask, update action items |\n| **Viewer** | Read-only inside the workspaces they belong to |\n\nOwners and Admins see every workspace. Everyone else, Team Leads included, sees only the workspaces they've been added to. Only an Org Owner or Org Admin can make someone a Team Lead.\n\nThe workspace you're in shows at the top of the pages that belong to one workspace (**Knowledge & docs**, **Meetings**, **Memory & gaps**). If you're in just one workspace it's a plain label; if you're in two or more it's a small menu to switch between them. Org-wide pages (Overview, Usage & billing, Users & access, Activity, Security & audit, Settings and the rest) don't show a workspace at all.\n\n## Sources and citations\nWhen Sidekick answers, it first searches the workspace for the passages most related to your question (by keyword and by meaning, so different wording still finds the right passage), then answers **only from those passages**. Every claim is tagged with its source, like **[S1]**, and the source list shows the document or meeting, the page or timestamp, and a short excerpt. If two sources disagree, Sidekick says so and cites both.\n\n## \"I couldn't find that\" and knowledge gaps\nIf the workspace doesn't contain the answer, Sidekick says so instead of guessing. The question is saved as a **knowledge gap** so someone can fill it in, for example by uploading the missing document. Gaps also come from questions nobody answered in a meeting, conflicting facts, out-of-date documents, and decisions that only exist in a transcript.\n\n## Guardrails\nRules Sidekick follows in every answer, everywhere (portal, Teams, the home assistant). Some are **locked** and always on: no hate, racism, bias, harassment, sexual content or help with harm; it can't be talked out of its rules (\"ignore your instructions\", \"pretend you have no rules\"); it never crosses into another workspace or organization; and it only states project facts it can cite, otherwise it says **\"I don't know\"**. The Org Owner can add stricter **switches** for the whole organization (swearing, small talk, judging people, HR/legal/medical questions, contact details, competitors). Each question is checked before it's answered, and each answer is checked again before you see it: an answer without a real source becomes \"I don't know\", swear words are masked, and contact details are hidden when that switch is on.\n\nOn top of that, Org Owners and Admins can:\n- make **one workspace stricter** than the rest by turning on extra switches just for it (for example \"No talk about competitors\" for one sensitive client). A workspace can never be looser than the organization.\n- write **custom rules** in plain language, such as \"Never quote day rates\", for the whole organization or one workspace (up to 20 each). Custom rules can only add restrictions: a rule that tries to loosen the guardrails (\"ignore the rules for managers\", \"you may swear\") is refused when it's saved, with the reason. Every version of a rule is kept.\n\nA question refused by a custom rule gets a fixed reply (\"Your organization has a rule that stops me from answering this here…\", pointing people to an Org Owner or Admin if that seems wrong) that doesn't repeat the rule, because client guests may see it in Teams.\n\n## Knowledge map and coverage\nOn **Memory & gaps**, Sidekick groups what a workspace knows into topics. Each topic has a **coverage** score from 0 to 100: high means it's well documented and consistent, low means it's mostly been talked about but not written down.\n\n## Meeting notes and action items\nImport a meeting transcript (a Teams `.vtt` file, a Word document or pasted text) and Sidekick writes the notes: a summary, decisions, **action items** (with owners and due dates as spoken), open questions, and lasting facts like requirements, risks and terms. Action items can be assigned and closed in the portal or straight from the card in Teams.\n\n## Internal and Shareable, and client guests\nEvery document is tagged:\n- **Internal**: for your team only (rates, staffing, internal risks).\n- **Shareable**: fine to use when the client is in the room.\n\nWhen **client guests are present** (in a meeting marked \"Client guests attended\", or a Teams channel marked as having client guests), Sidekick only uses Shareable sources. Notes from an internal-only meeting are never posted to a channel that has client guests.\n\n## Sidekick in Teams\nSidekick can be added to Teams channels and chats. Each channel or chat is **linked to one workspace** by an admin, and Sidekick only answers from that workspace there. See [Sidekick in Teams](teams-guide.md).\n\n## The in-app assistant\nThe chat box under the crystal ball on the home page. Ask it how Sidekick works (\"how do I import a meeting?\", \"what can a Viewer do?\") or ask it to do something: create a workspace, add a person, link a Teams chat, refresh a knowledge map, or open a page. While it thinks, the dust inside the ball swirls and the reply bubble shows what it's working on. It only offers what your role allows, and it never changes anything by itself: it shows a card with exactly what will happen, and nothing happens until you press **Confirm** (Cancel leaves everything as it was). Confirmed actions are recorded in the audit log like any other. For questions about a project's content, use **Ask Sidekick** on Knowledge & docs, which answers from your sources with citations. It only knows **your** organization and the workspaces you can use (names and counts, never document text), so it can't tell you about another client. Nobody can create a new client organization from the assistant, whatever their role: the Sidekick team sets those up in the Owner Console.\n\n## Audit log\nSign-ins, access changes, uploads, deletions, Teams links and every action Sidekick takes on its own are recorded on **Security & audit**, with who did it and when.\n\n## Usage and cost\nEach AI request is metered: how much text went in and out (tokens), what it cost, and how long it took. **Usage & billing** shows this by feature, workspace and day.\n\n## How your data is protected\n- Every request is checked: who you are, your role, and whether you belong to that workspace.\n- Every search and every AI request is limited to one organization and one workspace.\n- Documents and transcripts are treated as information, never as instructions, so text inside a file can't make Sidekick do something else.\n- Questions and answers go through your organization's **guardrails** (see above), and automated tests plant a secret in one workspace and check it can't be reached from another workspace, another organization, Teams or the assistant.\n- Sign in with your Microsoft work account (your company's password rules and MFA apply), or with a password, which is stored only as a secure hash. Your Org Owner can require Microsoft sign-in for everyone.\n",
  "admin-guide": "# Admin guide\n\nFor **Org Owners** and **Org Admins**: everything needed to set Sidekick up for your organization and keep it running. New to the terms? Read [Concepts](concepts.md) first.\n\n## 1. First sign-in\n**Where to sign in.** Use **Sign in** at the top of the Sidekick website. Once you're signed in, opening the website's home page takes you straight to your portal home (**Overview**). These guides are also on the website, under **Help**.\n\n**Signing up yourself.** When the Sidekick team has opened sign-up, a company can create its own organization at **/signup** (or **Start free trial** on the **/pricing** page): see [Sign-up, plan and billing](#15-sign-up-plan-and-billing) below.\n\nThe first person to open a new Sidekick install sets up the organization and becomes its **Org Owner**. If the Sidekick team onboarded you, sign in with the temporary password they gave you. Sidekick then shows only a **Pick your own password** screen until you choose a new one (at least 10 characters); nothing else works with a temporary password. Signing in with Microsoft instead also counts.\n\n**Signing in safely.** After 5 wrong passwords for one email (or 20 from one network address) within 15 minutes, password sign-in is paused for that email or address for 15 minutes, even with the right password. Wrong passwords on real accounts, the pause, sign-ins and sign-outs all appear on **Security & audit**. Org Owners and Org Admins see **Sign-in locked** next to a paused person on **Users & access** and can press **Unlock** to let them in straight away. Changing your password signs you out on every other browser and device. To sign out, use **Sign out** at the bottom of the sidebar, or tap your initials at the top right (the only way on a phone).\n\n## 2. Create groups\nA **group** (called a workspace in older screens) is one client team: its people, Teams chats and documents. **Groups → New group** sets one up on a single page:\n1. **Basics**: the group's name (e.g. \"Payments Platform\"), the **client** it's for (e.g. \"Northwind Bank\") and an optional engagement code. Names must be unique in your organization (ignoring upper/lower case).\n2. **Team Lead**: search for the person who runs the team day to day. If they're a Member or Viewer today, they become a **Team Lead** (the summary says so before you save, and it's in the audit log). Org Owners and Admins can't be a group's Team Lead; they already manage every group.\n3. **Members** (optional): tick people already in your organization. They keep their role. The Team Lead can add more later.\n4. **Teams chats**: assigned later, from the group's page (see section 7).\n\nYou're added to the group automatically. **Create group** opens the group's page.\n\n**The Groups page** shows a card per group with its client, Team Lead, and how many people, Teams chats, documents and meetings it has. A label says what needs attention, most urgent first: **No Team Lead**, **Setting up** (nobody, no chats and no documents added yet), **overdue action items**, **No documents**, or **Healthy**. **Needs attention** and **Setting up** filter the cards.\n\n**A group's page** (click its card) has tabs: **Overview** (a setup checklist and counts), **People** (add people already in your organization, or take someone off the group; their account and other groups stay), **Teams chats**, **Documents** (what Sidekick answers from; **Upload or manage** opens Knowledge & docs for that group) and **Settings**.\n\n**Rename or delete.** On the group's **Settings** tab, change its name, client or engagement code. **Delete group…** first shows what will go (documents, meetings, action items, asked questions, linked Teams chats) and asks you to confirm. Deleting removes everything in that workspace for good: documents and their search entries, meetings and their notes, questions and gaps, the knowledge map, its guardrail switches and custom rules, saved Teams messages and reminders. Linked Teams chats are unlinked. The audit log and usage records are kept. Renames, client and code changes and deletions are recorded on **Security & audit**.\n\n## 3. Turn on Microsoft sign-in (Org Owner)\n**Settings → Microsoft sign-in → Connect with Microsoft**, then sign in with your work account. This connects your organization to your company's Microsoft directory (tenant). From then on, people you've added can use **Sign in with Microsoft**. When everyone is set, switch on **Require Microsoft sign-in**: passwords stop working and anyone signed in with a password is signed out. You must have signed in with Microsoft yourself first, so you can't lock yourself out. **Disconnect** turns it off again (passwords work again).\n\n## 4. Add people\n**Users & access → Add person.** Pick their role and the workspaces they can see. Sidekick shows a **temporary password once**: share it with them privately; they must change it at first sign-in. Change roles or workspaces at any time from the same page. **Remove** asks you to confirm first. Nobody can change their own role or remove themselves (so you can't lock yourself out by accident): ask the Org Owner or another Org Admin. If your organization uses Microsoft sign-in, people can ignore the temporary password and use **Sign in with Microsoft** with that same email; the list shows a **Microsoft** badge once they have.\n\n**Import from Microsoft 365** (being added to each group's **People** tab). Once Microsoft sign-in is connected (step 3), you search your company's directory by name or email, tick the people, pick their role and groups, and import them. They're created as people who **sign in with Microsoft** (no temporary password); anyone already in Sidekick is just added to the groups you picked. Guests from other companies aren't listed. Team Leads can import too, as Member or Viewer into the groups they lead.\n\n**Approve Sidekick for your organization (once).** A **Microsoft admin of your company** (Global Administrator, Privileged Role Administrator or Cloud Application Administrator) approves Sidekick once: an Org Owner or Org Admin starts it from Sidekick, signs in on Microsoft's page as (or hands the link to) that admin, and approves. Until then importing and removing Sidekick from chats say Sidekick isn't approved yet. What the approval allows:\n- **Sign in and read user profile**: people sign in to Sidekick with their work account.\n- **Read all users' full profiles** (an *application* permission): Sidekick can search your directory when someone imports people or types `@Sidekick add` in Teams. It reads names, work emails, job titles and account ids only and never changes anything in Microsoft 365. It's an application permission (Sidekick reads as itself, not as you) because `@Sidekick add` happens in Teams, where nobody is signed in to the portal.\n- **Manage its own installation in chats and teams**: Sidekick can remove *itself* from a chat (never other apps): when someone who isn't a Team Lead or admin adds it, when nobody assigns a chat within 7 days, or when you remove it from a chat.\n- Reading Teams messages is **not** part of it: that's asked per chat or team when Sidekick is added there.\nThe approval is recorded on Security & audit; your Microsoft admin can withdraw it any time in the Microsoft Entra admin center (Enterprise applications → Sidekick).\n\n**Team Leads.** Give the person who runs a project the **Team Lead** role and add them to that project's workspace(s). They can then add their own team to those workspaces (as Member or Viewer), take people off, and assign the project's Teams chats, from their group's page, **Users & access** or the home assistant, without needing you. You can also make someone a Team Lead by picking them when you create a group. If they add someone who already has an account in your organization, that person is simply added to the lead's workspace. Roles, removing people from the organization and new workspaces stay with you.\n\n## 5. Add documents\n**Knowledge & docs**, in the workspace you want. Drop in PDF, Word (.docx), text, Markdown, CSV, JSON or Teams transcript (.vtt) files, up to 25 MB each and 20 at a time. Other file types and empty files are refused straight away with the reason, and aren't added to the list. A file with more than 2 million characters of text (about a 1,000-page book), a PDF over 2,000 pages, or a Word file that unpacks to far more than a real document holds is saved as **Failed** with the reason: split it into smaller files. Tag each one:\n- **Internal** for team-only material.\n- **Shareable** for anything fine to use in front of the client.\n\nYou can change the tag later; it takes effect immediately. **Delete** asks you to confirm first, then removes the document from search, so Sidekick can no longer use it.\n\n## 6. Import meetings\n**Meetings → Import transcript.** Upload the Teams transcript (`.vtt`), a Word file or paste the text, give it a title and date, and tick **Client guests attended** if the client was there. Notes are usually ready in 20–60 seconds.\n- **Limits:** files up to 25 MB, and the transcript text (pasted or from the file) up to 300,000 characters, about five hours of talk. The whole transcript goes to Claude in one request, so this keeps each import within what Claude can read at once and its cost predictable. Split a longer meeting into parts.\n- **If writing the notes fails** (for example, Claude was busy), the meeting is still saved: the dialog closes and the meeting opens with a **Failed** label and the reason. Choose **Try again** there; don't import it again.\n- **On the meeting's page:** the summary, decisions, open questions and facts, plus the **action items**, which you can edit right there like on Memory & gaps: tick them done, set the owner, pick a due date and change the status. The **Transcript** tab shows a long transcript in pieces (**Show more**).\n- **Delete** asks first: it removes the meeting together with its notes, action items and transcript.\n\nIf the workspace has linked Teams chats or channels, Sidekick posts the notes and action items there automatically.\n\n## 7. Link Teams chats and channels\nSee [Sidekick in Teams](teams-guide.md) for what people see. Once Microsoft sign-in is connected (step 3):\n1. **Only Team Leads and admins add Sidekick to a chat.** If anyone else adds it, Sidekick says so and leaves. (Recommended: in the Teams admin center, make the Sidekick app available only to your Team Leads and admins.)\n2. Sidekick posts one notice (\"I'm not set up for this chat yet\") and then reads, saves and answers **nothing** there until the chat is assigned.\n3. The chat shows as **waiting to be assigned** in the portal: on the group page of the Team Lead who added it, and for Org Owners and Org Admins. Pick the group (a Team Lead only their own groups), tick client guests if the client can read the chat, and assign it. Chats are assigned **only in the portal**, never from Teams.\n4. Once assigned, Sidekick reads **every message** in that chat (assigned means full access) and says so in the chat. Meeting notes and reminders stay switchable per chat.\n5. A chat nobody assigns within **7 days** is left automatically. **Remove Sidekick from this chat** on the group page makes Sidekick leave straight away and forget what it saved there.\n\n**With a code instead** (only where the above doesn't apply: before Microsoft sign-in is connected, and for 1:1 chats): Sidekick's card shows an 8-character code, valid for 24 hours (mention `@Sidekick link` for a new one). Open the group (**Groups**, then its card) → **Teams chats → Assign a chat**, enter the code, and tick **Client guests are in this chat or channel** if the client can read it.\n\nThe group's **Teams chats** tab lists its chats. Use the switch to change the client-guests setting, or **Unassign** to stop Sidekick answering and posting there (this also deletes the messages it saved from that chat).\n\nThe **Settings** button on each row of that list (Org Owners and Org Admins, and Team Leads for the workspaces they lead) chooses what Sidekick does in that chat; every change is in the audit log:\n- **Meeting notes**: post notes and action items here after each meeting (on by default). Turn it off in the other chats to keep notes in one dedicated chat.\n- **Keep track of every message** (channels and group chats linked with a code; chats assigned from the waiting list always track and can't switch it off): Sidekick reads every message, keeps it 90 days and uses it for answers, the knowledge map and reminders. It announces the change in the chat. This needs the Sidekick Teams app version 1.1 or later (it asks the team or chat owner for permission to read messages when it's added).\n- **Reminders**: a daily card at the time and time zone you pick, skipping the quiet days, plus one right after each meeting if you want. Choose which kinds: overdue or unowned action items, open questions and decisions, out-of-date documents, sources that disagree. **Preview** shows what the next one would say without posting; **Send now** posts it. The row shows when the last reminder went out (or why it failed).\n\nReminders only cover the chat's own workspace, and a chat with client guests only gets items whose sources are all Shareable. Out-of-date documents and disagreeing sources come from the knowledge map, so refresh it now and then (see below).\n\n## 8. Keep knowledge healthy\n**Memory & gaps → Refresh knowledge map** after adding material (Members and Admins can; Viewers and Billing Admins can't, since each refresh is a paid AI request). Work through the gaps: upload the missing document, or assign the gap to someone. Track action items in the same place: tick them done, set the owner, the due date (pick a date; a due date said in the meeting, like \"next Friday\", shows underneath until you do) and the status. Every change is in the audit log with what changed. The same edits work on the meeting's own page.\n\n## 9. Watch usage and activity\n- **Usage & billing**: your **plan** at the top (see section 15), then AI cost by feature, workspace and day for the whole month (also open to Billing Admins). Work that isn't tied to a workspace, such as the home assistant, shows as **Organization-wide**.\n- **Security & audit**: who did what and when, including everything Sidekick did on its own.\n\n## 10. See what Sidekick is doing (Activity)\n- **Home → Sidekick live:** a live picture of Sidekick and its connections (Teams, Claude, search, database) with their health, and who's active.\n- **Govern → Activity:** every request from the portal and Teams. **Traces** shows each one step by step (how long search, Claude and Teams delivery took, and why something failed, with its reference code). **Messages** lists what people asked and whether Sidekick found the answer. Kept for 30 days; no document text is stored. Teams steps after a meeting import only appear when there's a linked chat to post to, and a feature the Sidekick team paused or turned off counts as refused, not as a Sidekick or Claude error.\n\n## 11. Set your guardrails (Org Owner and Org Admins)\n**Govern → Guardrails** shows the rules Sidekick follows in every workspace, in the portal, in Teams and in the home assistant. Everyone can read it; who can change what is listed below.\n- **Always on (locked):** no hate, racism or bias; no harassment; no sexual content; no help with harm; it can't be talked out of its rules; it never crosses client walls; and it says \"I don't know\" instead of guessing. Nobody can turn these off.\n- **Organization switches:** extra restrictions only the **Org Owner** can turn on or off: refuse questions with swearing, stick to work, no judging or ranking people, steer HR/legal/medical questions to the right people, hide email addresses and phone numbers in answers, and no talk about competitors. Switches can only make Sidekick stricter. Every change is recorded on Security & audit.\n- **Custom rules for the whole organization** (Org Owners and Admins): type a short rule in your own words, like \"Never quote day rates\" or \"Don't discuss the 2027 reorganization\", and press **Add rule**. Sidekick checks the rule first: it can only add restrictions, so anything that would loosen the guardrails (\"ignore the rules when a manager asks\", \"you may swear\", \"it's fine to guess\") is refused with the reason, and nothing is saved. Up to 20 rules, 200 characters each. **Edit** saves a new version, **History** shows every version and who changed it, **Remove** switches it off but keeps its history. A rule only refuses a question when there's no useful answer that keeps to it: with \"Don't share budget figures\", \"Is the project on budget?\" still gets a yes or no, just without the figures. Rules about how to answer (\"Keep answers short\") are accepted too and only shape the answers. A rule so broad it would refuse every ordinary work question (\"Don't answer any questions\", \"Never discuss the project\") is refused as **too broad**: name the topic to avoid instead. A question refused by a custom rule gets the reply \"Your organization has a rule that stops me from answering this here\", with a pointer to an Org Owner or Admin; it never quotes the rule.\n- **Workspace rules** (pick a workspace from the list): the switches in force there. A switch that's on for the organization stays on and can't be turned off for one workspace; Org Owners and Admins can turn **more** switches on for that workspace only, and add custom rules that apply only there (another 20).\n- **Test it** (Org Owners and Admins): type a question to see whether Sidekick would refuse it in the chosen workspace, which rule would catch it and what people would see. Add a draft rule to try it before saving. Nothing is saved: the question isn't stored and isn't counted on the guardrail numbers (the Claude check is metered on Usage like any other).\n- **Last 30 days** (Org Owners and Admins): how often each rule refused a question or cleaned up an answer, and how many of those came from Teams. Only counts are kept, never the questions or answers. The same total shows on Activity.\n\nEvery change (organization switches, workspace switches, adding, editing or removing a rule) is recorded on **Security & audit**. Refused questions get a short, polite reply and aren't logged as knowledge gaps.\n\n## 12. Use the assistant for quick admin jobs\nOn the home page, type requests like \"create a workspace for Fabrikam, code FAB\", \"add Maria Chen maria@fabrikam.example as a Member of Fabrikam\" or \"link Teams code K7Q2 to Fabrikam\". Sidekick shows a card with the details; check them and press **Confirm**. When adding a person, the card shows their temporary password once: send it to them privately. If a detail is missing, the assistant asks for it first.\n\n## 13. Branding\n**Settings**: upload your logo and pick a brand color. They're applied across the portal.\n\n## 14. AI models & agent, and Connectors\n- **AI models & agent** (everyone can read it): which Claude model each feature uses (answers, meeting notes, knowledge map, assistant, guardrail check, reminders), how Sidekick is told to behave, and what's ready versus planned. Models are set by the Sidekick team; your documents and meetings are never used to train a model.\n- **Connectors**: the sources Sidekick can use. Available now: document upload, Teams transcripts and Teams chats and channels (assigned on each group's page). Outlook, SharePoint, Jira and the others are planned; each will connect with its own sign-in, without storing passwords.\n\n## 15. Sign-up, plan and billing\nPayments are in **test mode** for now: no money moves, prices are made up, and Sidekick **never asks for card or bank details** (a blue \"Test mode\" note shows wherever billing appears).\n\n**Signing up.** On **/signup** enter your organization's name, your name, work email, a password (at least 10 characters) and a plan (Starter, Team or Business; compare them on **/pricing**). You become the **Org Owner**, are signed in straight away and land on **checkout**. Each email can only have one Sidekick account, in any organization. Sign-up is **off until the Sidekick team opens it**: until then **/signup** says \"Sign-up isn't open yet\" and **/pricing** shows **Contact us** instead of **Start free trial**; contact them to get an organization set up. Too many sign-up attempts from one network (5 an hour) are paused for an hour.\n\n**Free trial and paying.** Every new organization gets a **14-day free trial**. On checkout you see the plan, the price per person, how many people you have and the total every 30 days, and one **Pay (test mode)** button. **Not now** keeps the trial; everyone sees a \"Free trial: N days left\" note at the top until you pay.\n\n**Setup wizard.** After checkout (paying or **Not now**) a new organization lands on **Get started** (**/get-started**), which walks you through first-time setup one step at a time:\n1. **Welcome**: your plan and trial, and how groups and Team Leads work.\n2. **Connect Microsoft 365** (Org Owner): sign in once with your work account so your people can use **Sign in with Microsoft** (the same as Settings → Microsoft sign-in). The page lists, in plain words, what Microsoft asks you to allow.\n3. **Add to Teams**: who should get the Sidekick app (recommended: only Team Leads and admins) and what your Teams admin does in the Teams admin center.\n4. **Rules**: a guardrails starting point. **Standard** is the usual defaults; **Strict** turns every switch on, for regulated clients. Only the Org Owner can change it; it's the same as the Guardrails page.\n5. **First groups**: a name, client and Team Lead for each client team. Pick the Team Lead from your organization or choose **Someone new…** to add them (they become a Team Lead and you get their temporary password once, to share privately).\n\nThen **Done** shows what's set and what's left, and **Finish setup** ends it. Any step can be skipped (**Do this later**) and you can come back: until setup is finished, Org Owners and Org Admins see a **Finish setting up Sidekick** banner with a **Continue setup** link. Finishing is in the audit log. Organizations set up by the Sidekick team don't get the banner.\n\n**Plan on Usage & billing** (Org Owners, Org Admins and Billing Admins see it; **only the Org Owner and Billing Admins** can change it):\n- **Current plan**, its status (Free trial, Active, Payment failed, Canceled) and dates; **people** used against the plan's limit and **workspaces** against its limit. A person is anyone with an account, any role.\n- **Pay now** (when not active) opens checkout. **Change plan…** switches straight away (the paid-until date stays); a plan you've outgrown (more people or workspaces than it allows) is refused with the numbers.\n- **Cancel plan** asks first. Sidekick stays fully usable until the end of what you paid for (or the trial), then turns read-only.\n- **Simulate a failed payment** (test mode only) shows what your team would see when a payment fails.\n- Every change is on **Security & audit** (kind \"Billing\").\n\n**When the plan isn't active.**\n| Status | What happens |\n|---|---|\n| Free trial | Full use until the trial ends, with a note at the top saying how many days are left. |\n| Payment failed | Full use for a **7-day grace period**, with a warning to fix it. |\n| Canceled | Full use until the end of what was paid for, with a note. |\n| Any of these after its date | **Read-only**: everyone can still sign in and see everything; nothing can be added or changed (refused with \"Sidekick is read-only for your organization because…\"), and Sidekick doesn't answer in the portal, Teams or the assistant, write meeting notes or send reminders. Nothing is deleted. Paying on Usage & billing turns everything back on at once. |\n\nOrganizations set up by the Sidekick team (or the very first one on an install) have no self-service plan: Usage & billing says the plan is managed by the Sidekick team.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n| You see | What to do |\n|---|---|\n| A dialog \"Something went wrong on our side\" with a reference code | It's on Sidekick's side, not yours. Try again; if it repeats, click **Copy details** and send it (with the reference code) to the Sidekick team. |\n| \"Refused by your organization's guardrails\" (or \"Your organization has a rule that stops me…\") on a normal work question | Paste the question into **Guardrails → Test it** to see which rule caught it. A switch may be stricter than you need (for example \"Stick to work\"), or a custom rule may be worded too broadly: edit it and test again. If a locked rule refused an ordinary question, send the wording to the Sidekick team. |\n| \"This would loosen Sidekick's guardrails\" when adding a rule | Custom rules can only add restrictions. Rewrite it as something Sidekick must not do, e.g. \"Never quote day rates\". |\n| \"This rule is too broad\" when adding a rule | The rule would refuse every work question. Name the topic, figure or document to avoid instead. To stop Sidekick answering altogether, ask the Sidekick team to pause it. |\n| Questions fail with \"Guardrail check is paused\" | The Sidekick team paused the check; Sidekick never answers without it. Try again later. |\n| A dialog \"Can't reach Sidekick\" | Your connection or Sidekick is down. Anything you saved is safe; try again in a minute. |\n| \"This page hit a problem\" | Click **Try again**, or use the menu. If it repeats, send the reference code to the Sidekick team. |\n| \"Claude isn't connected yet\" | The Sidekick team needs to configure the AI key. Contact them. |\n| \"… is paused by the Sidekick team\" | A feature was paused for maintenance. Try again later. |\n| \"… is turned off for your organization by the Sidekick team\" | Not an error: the Sidekick team switched that feature off for your organization. Org Owners: contact the Sidekick team to have it turned back on. Everyone else: ask your Org Owner. |\n| \"This organization's Sidekick access is paused\" | Contact the Sidekick team. |\n| \"Sidekick is read-only for your organization because…\" | The free trial ended, a failed payment wasn't fixed, or the plan was canceled. The Org Owner or a Billing Admin pays on **Usage & billing** to turn everything back on. |\n| \"Self-service sign-up isn't open yet\" | Sign-up is off until the Sidekick team opens it. Contact them (see Support) to get an organization set up. |\n| A Teams link code doesn't work | Codes expire after 24 hours. Mention `@Sidekick link` in Teams for a new one. A code from another company's Teams never works in your organization. |\n| A chat doesn't appear under *Waiting to be assigned* | Microsoft sign-in must be connected (Settings), and the chat must be in your company's Microsoft 365. 1:1 chats never wait; use their link code. |\n| \"Sidekick isn't approved for your Microsoft 365 organization yet\" when importing | A Microsoft admin of your company approves it once: **Approve Sidekick for your organization** (Org Owner or Org Admin). |\n| \"Sidekick isn't set up for your organization yet\" in Teams | Your company hasn't signed up, or Microsoft sign-in isn't connected yet (Settings). |\n| Sidekick answers \"I couldn't find that\" | The workspace doesn't have it yet. Upload the document; the question is already listed as a gap. |\n",
  "teams-guide": "# Sidekick in Teams\n\nSidekick works inside Microsoft Teams group chats, channels and 1:1 chats. It answers from **one client workspace** (the one the chat or channel is linked to), posts meeting notes after each meeting, and, where an admin turned it on, keeps track of the chat and reminds the team about what's still open.\n\n## Getting started\n1. Add Sidekick where your team works:\n   - **Group chat** (including a meeting's chat): open the chat, click **+** or **⋯ → Manage apps** at the top, add **Sidekick**.\n   - **Channel**: on the team, **⋯ → Manage team → Apps**, add **Sidekick**.\n\n   Teams asks the chat or team owner to let Sidekick **read messages** there. Accept it: that's what lets Sidekick follow the chat once it's assigned to a group (it reads nothing before that, and announces it when it starts).\n2. Sidekick posts one notice: **\"I'm not set up for this chat yet. A Team Lead needs to assign this chat to a group in the Sidekick portal.\"** Until then it doesn't read, save or answer anything there (mentioning it just repeats the notice).\n3. The Team Lead (or an admin) assigns the chat to a group in the portal. Sidekick then says it's assigned and from then on reads every message in the chat. Assigning happens only in the portal; `@Sidekick link` just shows which group a chat belongs to.\n\n**Who can add Sidekick:** only Team Leads and admins. If anyone else adds it, Sidekick posts \"Only a Team Lead or admin can add me to a chat, so I'm leaving\" and removes itself. A chat nobody assigns within 7 days is left too, with a short goodbye.\n\nIf your organization hasn't connected Microsoft sign-in to Sidekick yet (and in 1:1 chats), Sidekick shows an 8-character **link code** instead, which a Team Lead or admin enters on the group's page (**Teams chats → Assign a chat**).\n\n**Your organization isn't on Sidekick yet?** Sidekick replies \"Sidekick isn't set up for your organization yet\" with a link to sign up, and reads or keeps nothing from that chat.\n\n### Group chat or channel?\nBoth work the same for Sidekick; use whatever your team already uses.\n- **Group chat**: one running conversation. Sidekick's answers, notes, action-item cards and reminders appear in the chat in order.\n- **Channel**: posts with replies. Meeting notes are a new post and their action items go in that post's thread; reminders are a new post; answers are replies to the post that mentioned Sidekick.\n- A **meeting chat** (the chat of a scheduled Teams meeting) is a group chat to Sidekick, so it's a natural place for that meeting series' notes.\n- **Names on a group's Teams chats tab**: a group chat shows its name if someone gave it one. A channel shows as \"Team › Channel\"; a team's first channel shows only the team name until it's renamed, because Teams doesn't tell apps that channel's name (it's \"General\" unless someone renamed it).\n\n## Asking questions\nMention Sidekick with your question:\n\n> @Sidekick when is the wave 2 cutover and how many stores are in it?\n\nPick **Sidekick** from the list Teams shows while you type `@Sidek…`, so the name turns into a highlighted mention. If you just type \"@Sidekick\" without picking it, Teams doesn't treat it as a mention: Sidekick usually still answers when the message *starts* with \"@Sidekick\" (it can read the chat's messages since version 1.1.0 of the app), but picking it from the list always works.\n\nIn a 1:1 chat with Sidekick you don't need the mention. You get a card with:\n- the answer, with citations like **[S1]**;\n- each source: the document or meeting, the page or timestamp, and a short excerpt.\n\nIf the workspace doesn't contain the answer, Sidekick says so instead of guessing, and logs it as a knowledge gap for the team to fill.\n\nYour organization's **guardrails** apply in Teams exactly as in the portal. A question that breaks them (abuse, trying to get Sidekick to drop its rules, asking about another client, something your Org Owner switched off, or one of your organization's or workspace's custom rules) gets a short, polite refusal card instead of an answer.\n\n## Meeting notes\nAfter a meeting transcript is imported for the workspace, Sidekick posts:\n1. a **notes card**: summary, decisions and questions still open, with **Open in Sidekick** for the full notes;\n2. one **card per action item** (in a group chat they follow the notes; in a channel they're replies in the notes post's thread).\n\nOn an action-item card:\n- **Assign**: type a name, click Assign. Open items move to *In progress*.\n- **Mark done**: closes it. **Reopen** brings it back.\n\nChanges show on the card for everyone, in the portal's tracker, and in the audit log under your name.\n\n## Reminders\nIn chats where an Org Admin turned **Reminders** on, Sidekick posts a short **reminder card** once a day (at the chosen time, not on quiet days such as weekends) and right after each meeting. It lists up to six things that are still open:\n- action items that are **overdue** or have **no owner**;\n- **open questions** and **decisions still to make** that are due (or have been open for a week);\n- documents that look **out of date** compared with newer meetings or chat messages (for example the runbook says Nov 5 but the cutover moved to Nov 12);\n- **sources that disagree**.\n\nEvery point says where it comes from (the meeting, document or chat, with its date). Sidekick never posts a point it can't back up with a source, and it posts nothing when there's nothing to remind about.\n\nButtons on each point:\n- **Assign** (action items): type a name. **Mark done**: closes the action item, answers the question or decision, or resolves the knowledge gap.\n- **Snooze**: hides it from reminders for a week. **Not relevant**: hides it for good.\n\nChanges show on the card for everyone and go in the audit log under your name. A Viewer can't change items.\n\n## Chats Sidekick keeps track of\nChats assigned to a group in the portal (in an organization with Microsoft sign-in connected) are always tracked: assigned means full access, and Sidekick announces it when the chat is assigned. To stop it, a Team Lead or admin removes Sidekick from the chat. In chats linked with a code, an admin chooses:\n\nAn Org Admin can switch **Keep track of every message** on for a channel or group chat. Sidekick then posts a notice in that chat, reads every message there (not only @mentions) and remembers them for 90 days, so it can answer from them and notice when plans change. It doesn't reply to messages that don't mention it. Only the linked workspace's team can search what it learned; in a chat with client guests, what's said there counts as Shareable. When the switch is turned off, Sidekick says so in the chat; when the chat is unlinked, the saved messages are deleted.\n\n## Who you are in Teams\nOnce you've signed in to the Sidekick portal with Microsoft, Sidekick recognises you in Teams too: your questions and card clicks count under your Sidekick name and role. A **Viewer** can ask questions but can't assign or close action items. People in a linked chat who don't have a Sidekick account can still ask and use the cards, for that chat's workspace only.\n\n## Adding people from the chat\nIn a chat linked to a workspace, the workspace's **Team Lead** or an **Org Admin** can manage who's in it without leaving Teams:\n\n> @Sidekick add @Maria Lopez\n>\n> @Sidekick remove @Maria Lopez\n\nPick each person from Teams' mention list (so they become real mentions). Sidekick replies with a card saying what it did for each person:\n- **add**: puts them in this chat's workspace. Someone who isn't in Sidekick yet gets an account as a **Member** who signs in with their Microsoft work account (no password to hand out). Guests from other companies can't be added (they can still ask questions in the chat), and Org Owners and Org Admins already see every workspace.\n- **remove**: takes them off this workspace only; their Sidekick account stays (an Org Admin removes accounts on Users & access). Nobody can take themselves off, and a Team Lead only adds or removes Members and Viewers.\n\nSidekick needs to know who's asking: sign in to the Sidekick portal with Microsoft once. Anyone else gets a polite \"only this workspace's Team Lead or an Org Admin can…\". Every change is in the audit log (\"… from Teams\").\n\n## Commands\n| Type | Does |\n|---|---|\n| `@Sidekick <question>` | Answer with sources |\n| `@Sidekick link` | Show the link code, or which workspace this chat is linked to |\n| `@Sidekick add @name` | Add someone to this chat's workspace (Team Lead or Org Admin) |\n| `@Sidekick remove @name` | Take someone off this chat's workspace (Team Lead or Org Admin) |\n| `@Sidekick help` | Show what Sidekick can do here |\n\n## What Sidekick can and can't see\n- It only reads messages that mention it (every message in a 1:1 chat with it), unless an admin turned on **Keep track of every message** for that chat (Sidekick announces it there).\n- Meeting notes go to the chats an admin left notes switched on for (all linked chats by default), so a workspace can keep them in one dedicated notes chat.\n- It only answers from the linked workspace. It never uses other workspaces or other clients' material.\n- In chats and channels marked as having **client guests**, it only uses documents tagged *Shareable*, never posts notes from internal-only meetings there, and only reminds about items whose every source is Shareable.\n- If the chat or channel is unlinked, Sidekick stops answering and posting there until it's linked again.\n",
  "roles-and-permissions": "# Roles and permissions\n\nEvery person in a Sidekick organization has exactly one of six roles. The role decides **what they can do**;\nthe workspaces they're added to decide **which client work they can see**. This page is the rulebook.\n\n## The roles\n\n| Role | In one line | Sees which workspaces |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Org Owner** | Runs the whole organization, including sign-in and the company-wide guardrails. One per organization. | All |\n| **Org Admin** | Runs people, workspaces and Teams links day to day. | All |\n| **Team Lead** | A Member who also runs the people side of the workspaces they lead: adds Members and Viewers, takes them off, links Teams chats. Made by Org Owners/Admins. | Only ones they're added to (the ones they lead) |\n| **Billing Admin** | Watches usage and AI spend, and pays for, changes or cancels the plan. Changes no content. | Only ones they're added to (usually none) |\n| **Member** | Does the client work: uploads, imports meetings, asks, updates action items. | Only ones they're added to |\n| **Viewer** | Reads and asks, never changes anything. | Only ones they're added to |\n\nSeparately, the **Sidekick team** uses the **Owner Console** (its own accounts, not a role in any\norganization) to set up client organizations and watch platform health. It never sees client documents.\n\n## Who can do what\n\n✓ = allowed. Workspace actions also need the person to be in that workspace (Org Owners and Org Admins\nare in all of them). Anything not allowed is refused with a message naming who can do it, e.g. \"Only the\nOrg Owner can do this\" or \"Only Org Owners, Org Admins and Billing Admins can do this\". Pages a role can't\nuse (Usage & billing, Activity, Security & audit) show the same kind of message instead of their content.\n\n| What | Org Owner | Org Admin | Team Lead | Billing Admin | Member | Viewer |\n|---|:-:|:-:|:-:|:-:|:-:|:-:|\n| **Everyone signed in** | | | | | | |\n| See the home page, their profile and their groups (workspaces) | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ |\n| See who's in their groups (emails: admins and the group's Team Lead only) | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ |\n| See live status on the home page (names of who's active: admins only) | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ |\n| Use the home assistant (it only offers actions their role allows) | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ |\n| See the guardrails in force | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ |\n| **Inside their workspaces: read** | | | | | | |\n| Read documents, meetings, notes and the knowledge map | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ |\n| Ask Sidekick questions (portal and Teams) | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ |\n| **Inside their workspaces: change** | | | | | | |\n| Upload, rename or delete documents | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | | ✓ | |\n| Import, reprocess or delete meetings | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | | ✓ | |\n| Update action items (portal and Teams card buttons) | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | | ✓ | |\n| Refresh the knowledge map, resolve or dismiss gaps | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | | ✓ | |\n| **Money** | | | | | | |\n| See their own AI use this month on the home page | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ |\n| See Usage & billing and the whole company's AI spend | ✓ | ✓ | | ✓ | | |\n| See the plan and subscription (on Usage & billing) | ✓ | ✓ | | ✓ | | |\n| Pay for the plan, change it or cancel it | ✓ | | | ✓ | | |\n| **Running the organization** | | | | | | |\n| Create groups (workspaces), picking their client, Team Lead and first members | ✓ | ✓ | | | | |\n| Rename a group, change its client or engagement code, delete it (and everything in it) | ✓ | ✓ | | | | |\n| Add people to their groups or take them off, change which of them a person is in | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ (Members and Viewers, own groups; not themselves) | | | |\n| Change roles, remove people from the organization | ✓ | ✓ | | | | |\n| Link, change or unlink Teams channels and chats | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ (own workspaces) | | | |\n| Add Sidekick to a Teams chat (anyone else: Sidekick leaves) | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | | | |\n| See and assign Teams chats waiting to be assigned (portal only) | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ (chats they added, to own groups) | | | |\n| Remove Sidekick from a Teams chat | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ (own groups' chats, or waiting chats they added) | | | |\n| Import people from Microsoft 365 | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ (Members and Viewers, own workspaces) | | | |\n| `@Sidekick add` / `remove @name` in a linked Teams chat | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ (Members and Viewers, that chat's workspace if they lead it) | | | |\n| Approve Sidekick for the company's Microsoft 365 (starts Microsoft's admin approval) | ✓ | ✓ | | | | |\n| See the audit log and Activity (traces, performance) | ✓ | ✓ | | | | |\n| Change branding (logo, brand color) | ✓ | ✓ | | | | |\n| Make a workspace's guardrails stricter, write custom rules, use \"Test it\" | ✓ | ✓ | | | | |\n| **Org Owner only** | | | | | | |\n| Turn company-wide guardrail switches on or off | ✓ | | | | | |\n| Connect, require or disconnect Microsoft sign-in | ✓ | | | | | |\n\n## Rules that always hold\n\n- **Walls first.** Nobody sees a workspace they aren't in; asking for one returns \"not found\", so people\n  can't even tell it exists. Organizations never see each other at all.\n- **Role before data.** Every change checks the role first, so a refused person learns nothing about\n  what exists.\n- **No one makes an Org Owner** from Users & access, and the Org Owner can't be changed or removed there.\n  Org Admins can add other Org Admins and make Team Leads (also by picking a Member or Viewer as a new group's Team Lead).\n- **Nobody changes their own role or removes themselves.** An Org Admin who wants a different role, or to\n  leave, asks the Org Owner or another Org Admin, so nobody locks themselves out by accident.\n- **Temporary passwords first.** Someone added with a temporary password can do nothing but choose a new\n  password (or sign out) until they do; every other request is refused. Signing in with Microsoft clears it.\n- **Password guessing is slowed down.** 5 wrong passwords for one email (or 20 from one network address)\n  in 15 minutes pause password sign-in for 15 minutes. Failed sign-ins and sign-outs go to the audit log.\n  Users & access marks a paused person **Sign-in locked**; an Org Owner or Org Admin can press **Unlock**\n  (audited), for example when someone else's guessing locked them out.\n- **A new password signs you out everywhere else.** Changing your password ends your sessions on every other\n  browser and device, and the new password must differ from the old one.\n- **Changes only from Sidekick's own pages.** A change (anything but reading) sent by another website is\n  refused, and Sidekick can't be shown inside another site's frame.\n- **Team Leads stay inside their workspaces.** They only see, add and take off people in the workspaces\n  they lead, can only give the Member or Viewer role, and can't change roles, remove anyone from the\n  organization, create workspaces, or open admin pages.\n- **AI use is personal unless you handle billing.** Every AI call is recorded with the person it was for\n  (in the portal, or from a linked Microsoft account in Teams). Members and Viewers see only their own;\n  Org Owners, Org Admins and Billing Admins see the company total. Work Sidekick does on its own, and\n  questions from Teams guests, count only toward the company total.\n- **A lapsed plan makes everything read-only, for every role.** When a free trial ends unpaid, a failed\n  payment isn't fixed within 7 days, or a canceled plan runs out, everyone can still sign in and see\n  everything, but nothing can be added or changed and Sidekick doesn't answer (portal, Teams, assistant).\n  Org Owners and Billing Admins can still pay, which turns everything back on.\n- **Client guests** in a Teams chat only ever get answers from Shareable documents, whatever the asker's role.\n- **Same rules everywhere.** The portal, Teams and the home assistant use the same checks; the assistant\n  never offers an action the role can't do, and the server refuses it again if it tried.\n- **Guardrails can only get stricter** below the company level: a workspace or a custom rule can add\n  limits, never remove the locked floor.\n\n",
};
